ENTRY 12445 {"id":12445,"kind":"pi.tool-result","messages":[{"role":"toolResult","text":"     1\t#!/usr/bin/env bash\n     2\t# Apply the OneCLI v2 host allowlist (docs/onecli-host-rules.md): one `allow` org rule per host, then `block *`.\n     3\t# Default is a dry run that only prints the plan and makes no request. --apply writes through the OneCLI API.\n     4\t#\n     5\t#   deploy/onecli-host-rules.sh            # plan only\n     6\t#   deploy/onecli-host-rules.sh --apply    # create the missing rules, block last\n     7\t#\n     8\t# The admin key (`oc_...`) is read from Secret secret-store/pi-onecli-admin, key api-key, with kubectl (read only), or\n     9\t# from ONECLI_API_KEY. It is passed to curl through a config on stdin: never printed, never on a command line.\n    10\t# ONECLI_URL is the API base (required, e.g. https://onecli.example.com; or http://localhost:10256 after\n    11\t# `kubectl -n onecli port-forward svc/onecli 10256`). KUBE_CONTEXT defaults to the current kubectl context.\n    12\t#\n    13\t# Rules are matched by name (`pi-allow-<host>`, `pi-block-all`), so a rerun only adds what is missing. Order matters:\n    14\t# the block rule must come after every allow rule. Missing allow rules are created first, while the old block still\n    15\t# exists (a new allow rule is then at worst ineffective, never a hole). Only then is the block deleted and recreated\n    16\t# last: the one step with a window, and an EXIT trap recreates the block if anything fails inside it. Rules written here are live at once and change what every OneCLI agent\n    17\t# can reach, so run this only when the hosts below are complete for the work in flight.\n    18\tset -euo pipefail\n    19\t\n    20\tapply=0\n    21\t[[ ${1:-} == --apply ]] && apply=1\n    22\tbase=${ONECLI_URL:?set ONECLI_URL to the OneCLI API base, e.g. https://onecli.example.com}\n    23\tctx=${KUBE_CONTEXT:-}\n    24\trules_path=/v1/org/policy/rules\n    25\t\n    26\t# Keep in step with deploy/onecli-policies/egress.yaml (the pod's FQDN allowlist).\n    27\thosts=(\n    28\t  chatgpt.com '*.chatgpt.com' auth.openai.com api.openai.com openrouter.ai\n    29\t  github.com api.github.com codeload.github.com objects.githubusercontent.com\n    30\t  registry.npmjs.org pypi.org files.pythonhosted.org\n    31\t)\n    32\tblock_name=pi-block-all\n    33\t\n    34\trule_name() { printf 'pi-allow-%s' \"${1//\\*./wild-}\"; }\n    35\t\n    36\tkey=${ONECLI_API_KEY:-}\n    37\tif [[ -z $key ]]; then\n    38\t  key=$(kubectl ${ctx:+--context=\"$ctx\"} -n secret-store get secret pi-onecli-admin -o jsonpath='{.data.api-key}' | base64 -d)\n    39\tfi\n    40\t[[ -n $key ]] || { echo \"no admin key (secret-store/pi-onecli-admin api-key or ONECLI_API_KEY)\" >&2; exit 1; }\n    41\t\n    42\tapi() { # method path [json body]: prints the response body, fails on HTTP >= 400\n    43\t  local method=$1 path=$2 body=${3:-}\n    44\t  local args=(-sS --fail-with-body -X \"$method\" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -K - \"$base$path\")\n    45\t  [[ -n $body ]] && args+=(--data \"$body\")\n    46\t  printf 'header = \"Authorization: Bearer %s\"\\n' \"$key\" | curl \"${args[@]}\"\n    47\t}\n    48\trule_json() { # name action host\n    49\t  jq -nc --arg n \"$1\" --arg a \"$2\" --arg h \"$3\" '{name:$n, action:$a, targets:[{kind:\"network\", hostPattern:$h}]}'\n    50\t}\n    51\t\n    52\tif [[ $apply -eq 1 ]]; then\n    53\t  existing=$(api GET \"$rules_path\")\n    54\telse\n    55\t  existing='[]'\n    56\t  echo \"dry run: not contacting OneCLI, so every rule is shown as missing. Use --apply to write.\"\n    57\tfi\n    58\t# The list is a bare array on v2.20.0; the wrapped forms are accepted in case a later version changes it.\n    59\tnames=$(jq -r '(if type==\"array\" then . else (.rules // .data // []) end)[].name' <<<\"$existing\")\n    60\tblock_id=$(jq -r --arg n \"$block_name\" '(if type==\"array\" then . else (.rules // .data // []) end)[] | select(.name==$n) | .id' <<<\"$existing\" | head -1)\n    61\thas() { grep -qxF -- \"$1\" <<<\"$names\"; }\n    62\t\n    63\tmissing=()\n    64\tfor h in \"${hosts[@]}\"; do has \"$(rule_name \"$h\")\" || missing+=(\"$h\"); done\n    65\t\n    66\tfor h in ${missing[@]+\"${missing[@]}\"}; do\n    67\t  echo \"plan: allow $h\"\n    68\t  [[ $apply -eq 1 ]] && api POST \"$rules_path\" \"$(rule_json \"$(rule_name \"$h\")\" allow \"$h\")\" >/dev/null\n    69\tdone\n    70\tblock_gone=0\n    71\trestore_block() { # EXIT trap: never leave the allowlist off after the block was deleted\n    72\t  local rc=$?\n    73\t  trap - EXIT\n    74\t  if [[ $block_gone -eq 1 ]]; then\n    75\t    echo \"failed after deleting $block_name: recreating it\" >&2\n    76\t    api POST \"$rules_path\" \"$(rule_json \"$block_name\" block '*')\" >/dev/null ||\n    77\t      echo \"could not recreate $block_name; rerun with --apply NOW (the allowlist is off)\" >&2\n    78\t  fi\n    79\t  exit \"$rc\"\n    80\t}\n    81\ttrap restore_block EXIT\n    82\tif [[ -n $block_id && ${#missing[@]} -gt 0 ]]; then\n    83\t  echo \"plan: delete $block_name ($block_id) and recreate it last, after the new allow rules\"\n    84\t  if [[ $apply -eq 1 ]]; then\n    85\t    api DELETE \"$rules_path/$block_id\" >/dev/null # an HTTP error here means nothing was deleted\n    86\t    block_gone=1\n    87\t  fi\n    88\t  block_id=\"\"\n    89\tfi\n    90\tif [[ -z $block_id ]]; then\n    91\t  echo \"plan: block * (last)\"\n    92\t  if [[ $apply -eq 1 ]]; then\n    93\t    block_gone=1\n    94\t    api POST \"$rules_path\" \"$(rule_json \"$block_name\" block '*')\" >/dev/null\n    95\t    block_gone=0\n    96\t  fi\n    97\tfi\n    98\ttrap - EXIT\n    99\t[[ $apply -eq 1 ]] && echo \"done: ${#hosts[@]} allow rules, then block *. Verify with docs/onecli-host-rules.md.\" || true\n","isError":false}]}
ENTRY 12479 {"id":12479,"kind":"pi.assistant","messages":[{"role":"assistant","text":"[thinking]","calls":["bash({\"command\":\"cd /workspace/repo && cat -n docs/onecli-host-rules.md && rg -n 'onecli-host-rules|hostPattern|policy/rules'\"})"]}]}
ENTRY 12481 {"id":12481,"kind":"pi.tool-result","messages":[{"role":"toolResult","text":"     1\t# OneCLI v2 host rules (public egress allowlist)\n     2\t\n     3\tWhy: OneCLI v2.20.0 refuses private, cluster and metadata targets, but it relays **any public host** for a valid agent\n     4\ttoken (`docs/research/onecli-v2-recheck.md`, section 2). The `Default Rule: block` switch (`/v1/org/policy/default`) does\n     5\tnot change that: it only applies to credentialed non-LLM requests. The working allowlist is ordered explicit org rules:\n     6\t`allow <host>` for each host, then `block *`. That test returned 403 for every host not allowed and 200 with injection for\n     7\t`openrouter.ai`.\n     8\t\n     9\tThis is the gateway-side control (what a leaked token can ask for). The pod-side control is the Cilium FQDN allowlist\n    10\tin `deploy/onecli-policies/egress.yaml`; keep both lists identical.\n    11\t\n    12\t## The rule set\n    13\t\n    14\tRule shape (recheck doc): `{\"name\", \"action\": \"allow|block\", \"targets\": [{\"kind\": \"network\", \"hostPattern\"}]}`, at\n    15\t`POST /v1/org/policy/rules`, effective immediately. No `pathPattern` or `method`: path rules are bypassable (`..`,\n    16\t`/repositories/<id>`), so the boundary is the host. Repository scope comes from the per-session GitHub App token, not from here.\n    17\t\n    18\t| # | action | hostPattern | Why |\n    19\t|---|---|---|---|\n    20\t| 1 | allow | `chatgpt.com` | Codex subscription calls (credential injected) |\n    21\t| 2 | allow | `*.chatgpt.com` | same, subdomains |\n    22\t| 3 | allow | `auth.openai.com` | OAuth token refresh |\n    23\t| 4 | allow | `api.openai.com` | OpenAI API |\n    24\t| 5 | allow | `openrouter.ai` | OpenRouter models |\n    25\t| 6 | allow | `github.com` | git clone and push over HTTPS (Phase 4c) |\n    26\t| 7 | allow | `api.github.com` | GitHub API, installation-token mint if it goes through the gateway |\n    27\t| 8 | allow | `codeload.github.com` | archive downloads |\n    28\t| 9 | allow | `objects.githubusercontent.com` | release and LFS objects |\n    29\t| 10 | allow | `registry.npmjs.org` | npm |\n    30\t| 11 | allow | `pypi.org` | pip index |\n    31\t| 12 | allow | `files.pythonhosted.org` | pip files |\n    32\t| 13 | block | `*` | everything else, **created last** |\n    33\t\n    34\tOrder is the point: rule 13 must be created after rules 1 to 12. The script keeps that invariant (see below).\n    35\tWhether the gateway evaluates in creation order or by specificity is UNVERIFIED beyond the recheck test (allow then block\n    36\tbehaved as an allowlist); check with the probes below after applying.\n    37\t\n    38\t## Apply\n    39\t\n    40\t`deploy/onecli-host-rules.sh`, applied on c2 on 2026-11-16 (the org had no rules before). Dry run by default.\n    41\t\n    42\t```sh\n    43\tdeploy/onecli-host-rules.sh            # prints the plan, makes no request\n    44\tdeploy/onecli-host-rules.sh --apply    # needs the admin key\n    45\t```\n    46\t\n    47\t- Admin key: Secret `secret-store/pi-onecli-admin`, key `api-key`, read with `kubectl` (or `ONECLI_API_KEY`). It goes to\n    48\t  curl on stdin and is never printed. This is the admin key the plan says must never live in the orchestrator: run the\n    49\t  script from an operator machine only.\n    50\t- `ONECLI_URL` (required): the OneCLI API base, e.g. `https://onecli.example.com`. Use `http://localhost:10256` with\n    51\t  `kubectl -n onecli port-forward svc/onecli 10256` if the Cilium ingress policy has closed the route.\n    52\t- Idempotent by rule name (`pi-allow-<host>`, `pi-block-all`). Adding a host later: add it to `hosts` in the script and to\n    53\t  `egress.yaml`, rerun; the script creates the missing allow rules first, while `pi-block-all` still exists, then deletes\n    54\t  and recreates the block last. If creating an allow rule fails, the old block is untouched (the allowlist stays on).\n    55\t  Only between the block DELETE and its POST is there no block rule (the gateway relays public hosts as before): an\n    56\t  EXIT trap recreates the block if anything fails in that step, and prints a \"rerun with --apply NOW\" message if even\n    57\t  that fails. Do it when no session is running. Whether the gateway evaluates by creation order is UNVERIFIED, so a\n    58\t  new allow rule created before the block is recreated may be ineffective until the block is recreated last; that is\n    59\t  fail-closed.\n    60\t- The list response is a bare array (seen on 2026-11-16, empty before the first apply). UNVERIFIED: `DELETE\n    61\t  /v1/org/policy/rules/<id>` and the id field (not exercised yet).\n    62\t\n    63\t## Verify after applying\n    64\t\n    65\tFrom a pod that holds an agent token (the orchestrator has one; do not print it), through `:10255`:\n    66\t\n    67\t- `https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/key` returns 200 with the secret injected.\n    68\t- `https://example.com` and `https://api.ipify.org` return 403.\n    69\t- `https://github.com/<public repo>.git/info/refs?service=git-upload-pack` returns 200 (a private repo needs the session token).\n    70\t\n    71\t## Result (2026-11-16)\n    72\t\n    73\tProbes from the orchestrator pod through `:10255` with its agent token (not printed), right after `--apply`:\n    74\t\n    75\t| Target | Status |\n    76\t|---|---|\n    77\t| `https://example.com/` | 403 |\n    78\t| `https://api.ipify.org/` | 403 |\n    79\t| `https://github.com/<owner>/<repo-a>.git/info/refs?service=git-upload-pack` | 200 |\n    80\t| `https://registry.npmjs.org/left-pad` | 200 |\n    81\t| `https://chatgpt.com/` | 200 |\n    82\t\n    83\tThe Cilium FQDN policy on the OneCLI pod (argo-c2 `applications/onecli`) is the second layer: a host allowed here but\n    84\tmissing there fails to connect.\n    85\t\n    86\tSandboxes still reach public hosts directly (`sandbox-allow-internet-egress`, decision 2026-11-15), so this list only\n    87\tlimits what goes through the gateway: model calls and git with injected credentials. Routing sandbox traffic through the\n    88\tgateway too would need `HTTPS_PROXY` in the sandbox, the OneCLI CA in every tool, and the package hosts each session\n    89\tneeds on this list. It is not done; revisit before real use with untrusted code.\n    90\t\n    91\t## Rollback\n    92\t\n    93\tList rules with `GET /v1/org/policy/rules` and delete `pi-block-all` first (restores relaying of public hosts as in\n    94\tv2.20.0 defaults), then the `pi-allow-*` rules if wanted. Deleting only the block rule is enough to undo the restriction.\n/bin/bash: line 1: rg: command not found\n<harness>\n[error] Command exited with code 127\n</harness>","isError":true}]}
ENTRY 12488 {"id":12488,"kind":"pi.assistant","messages":[{"role":"assistant","text":"[thinking]","calls":["bash({\"command\":\"cd /workspace/repo && sed -n '32,99p' deploy/onecli-host-rules.sh && cat -n deploy/onecli-policies/egress.yaml | head -100\"})"]}]}
ENTRY 12490 {"id":12490,"kind":"pi.tool-result","messages":[{"role":"toolResult","text":"block_name=pi-block-all\n\nrule_name() { printf 'pi-allow-%s' \"${1//\\*./wild-}\"; }\n\nkey=${ONECLI_API_KEY:-}\nif [[ -z $key ]]; then\n  key=$(kubectl ${ctx:+--context=\"$ctx\"} -n secret-store get secret pi-onecli-admin -o jsonpath='{.data.api-key}' | base64 -d)\nfi\n[[ -n $key ]] || { echo \"no admin key (secret-store/pi-onecli-admin api-key or ONECLI_API_KEY)\" >&2; exit 1; }\n\napi() { # method path [json body]: prints the response body, fails on HTTP >= 400\n  local method=$1 path=$2 body=${3:-}\n  local args=(-sS --fail-with-body -X \"$method\" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -K - \"$base$path\")\n  [[ -n $body ]] && args+=(--data \"$body\")\n  printf 'header = \"Authorization: Bearer %s\"\\n' \"$key\" | curl \"${args[@]}\"\n}\nrule_json() { # name action host\n  jq -nc --arg n \"$1\" --arg a \"$2\" --arg h \"$3\" '{name:$n, action:$a, targets:[{kind:\"network\", hostPattern:$h}]}'\n}\n\nif [[ $apply -eq 1 ]]; then\n  existing=$(api GET \"$rules_path\")\nelse\n  existing='[]'\n  echo \"dry run: not contacting OneCLI, so every rule is shown as missing. Use --apply to write.\"\nfi\n# The list is a bare array on v2.20.0; the wrapped forms are accepted in case a later version changes it.\nnames=$(jq -r '(if type==\"array\" then . else (.rules // .data // []) end)[].name' <<<\"$existing\")\nblock_id=$(jq -r --arg n \"$block_name\" '(if type==\"array\" then . else (.rules // .data // []) end)[] | select(.name==$n) | .id' <<<\"$existing\" | head -1)\nhas() { grep -qxF -- \"$1\" <<<\"$names\"; }\n\nmissing=()\nfor h in \"${hosts[@]}\"; do has \"$(rule_name \"$h\")\" || missing+=(\"$h\"); done\n\nfor h in ${missing[@]+\"${missing[@]}\"}; do\n  echo \"plan: allow $h\"\n  [[ $apply -eq 1 ]] && api POST \"$rules_path\" \"$(rule_json \"$(rule_name \"$h\")\" allow \"$h\")\" >/dev/null\ndone\nblock_gone=0\nrestore_block() { # EXIT trap: never leave the allowlist off after the block was deleted\n  local rc=$?\n  trap - EXIT\n  if [[ $block_gone -eq 1 ]]; then\n    echo \"failed after deleting $block_name: recreating it\" >&2\n    api POST \"$rules_path\" \"$(rule_json \"$block_name\" block '*')\" >/dev/null ||\n      echo \"could not recreate $block_name; rerun with --apply NOW (the allowlist is off)\" >&2\n  fi\n  exit \"$rc\"\n}\ntrap restore_block EXIT\nif [[ -n $block_id && ${#missing[@]} -gt 0 ]]; then\n  echo \"plan: delete $block_name ($block_id) and recreate it last, after the new allow rules\"\n  if [[ $apply -eq 1 ]]; then\n    api DELETE \"$rules_path/$block_id\" >/dev/null # an HTTP error here means nothing was deleted\n    block_gone=1\n  fi\n  block_id=\"\"\nfi\nif [[ -z $block_id ]]; then\n  echo \"plan: block * (last)\"\n  if [[ $apply -eq 1 ]]; then\n    block_gone=1\n    api POST \"$rules_path\" \"$(rule_json \"$block_name\" block '*')\" >/dev/null\n    block_gone=0\n  fi\nfi\ntrap - EXIT\n[[ $apply -eq 1 ]] && echo \"done: ${#hosts[@]} allow rules, then block *. Verify with docs/onecli-host-rules.md.\" || true\n     1\t# Where the OneCLI pod may connect. Writing an egress rule makes everything not listed here denied, so the\n     2\t# cluster, the LAN and the node network are closed by default; the only exceptions are below.\n     3\t#\n     4\t#   DNS     kube-dns, with a DNS proxy rule: toFQDNs only works for names Cilium saw resolved through it.\n     5\t#   DB      the onecli database on the shared CNPG cluster (DATABASE_URL points at pg-db-cluster-rw.postgres-clusters:5432).\n     6\t#   FQDNs   the host allowlist, :443 only. Keep it in step with the OneCLI host rules (docs/onecli-host-rules.md):\n     7\t#           the gateway rules say what a token may ask for, this says where the pod can physically go.\n     8\t# The egressDeny block is defence in depth: a public name that resolves to a private address (DNS rebinding) must\n     9\t# not be reachable even if it is on the list. The gateway already refuses private targets (v2.20.0); this does not\n    10\t# depend on it. Deny wins over allow, so the cluster CIDRs must not swallow the Postgres pod: CIDR rules do not match\n    11\t# pod endpoints by default, but that is UNVERIFIED on c2 until Cilium runs. Check with `hubble observe` first.\n    12\t#\n    13\t# UNVERIFIED: whether web, api or gateway call anything else (update checks, telemetry, ONECLI_EXTERNAL_URL\n    14\t# https://onecli.example.com from inside the pod). Roll out with policy audit mode on the pod, read Hubble for\n    15\t# would-be drops, then enforce.\n    16\tapiVersion: cilium.io/v2\n    17\tkind: CiliumNetworkPolicy\n    18\tmetadata:\n    19\t  name: onecli-egress\n    20\t  namespace: onecli\n    21\tspec:\n    22\t  endpointSelector:\n    23\t    matchLabels:\n    24\t      app: onecli\n    25\t  egressDeny:\n    26\t    - toCIDRSet:\n    27\t        - cidr: 10.0.0.0/8\n    28\t        - cidr: 172.16.0.0/12\n    29\t        - cidr: 192.168.0.0/16\n    30\t        - cidr: 100.64.0.0/10\n    31\t        - cidr: 169.254.0.0/16\n    32\t  egress:\n    33\t    - toEndpoints:\n    34\t        - matchLabels:\n    35\t            k8s:io.kubernetes.pod.namespace: kube-system\n    36\t            k8s-app: kube-dns\n    37\t      toPorts:\n    38\t        - ports:\n    39\t            - { port: \"53\", protocol: UDP }\n    40\t            - { port: \"53\", protocol: TCP }\n    41\t          rules:\n    42\t            dns:\n    43\t              - matchPattern: \"*\"\n    44\t    - toEndpoints:\n    45\t        - matchLabels:\n    46\t            k8s:io.kubernetes.pod.namespace: postgres-clusters\n    47\t            cnpg.io/cluster: pg-db-cluster\n    48\t      toPorts:\n    49\t        - ports:\n    50\t            - { port: \"5432\", protocol: TCP }\n    51\t    - toFQDNs:\n    52\t        # Model providers (ChatGPT/Codex subscription, OpenAI API, OpenRouter).\n    53\t        - matchName: chatgpt.com\n    54\t        - matchPattern: \"*.chatgpt.com\"\n    55\t        - matchName: auth.openai.com\n    56\t        - matchName: api.openai.com\n    57\t        - matchName: openrouter.ai\n    58\t        # GitHub (Phase 4c clone and push).\n    59\t        - matchName: github.com\n    60\t        - matchName: api.github.com\n    61\t        - matchName: codeload.github.com\n    62\t        - matchName: objects.githubusercontent.com\n    63\t        # Package registries.\n    64\t        - matchName: registry.npmjs.org\n    65\t        - matchName: pypi.org\n    66\t        - matchName: files.pythonhosted.org\n    67\t      toPorts:\n    68\t        - ports:\n    69\t            - { port: \"443\", protocol: TCP }\n","isError":false}]}
ENTRY 12494 {"id":12494,"kind":"pi.assistant","messages":[{"role":"assistant","text":"## Findings\n\n### 1. A lost DELETE response can leave the gateway without a block rule\n- Severity: high\n- Confidence: high\n- Location: deploy/onecli-host-rules.sh:85\n- What happens: If OneCLI deletes the block rule but the response is lost or curl otherwise fails, the script exits before setting `block_gone`. The EXIT trap therefore does not restore the rule, leaving public hosts unrestricted by this allowlist.\n- Evidence: `api DELETE \"$rules_path/$block_id\" >/dev/null` precedes `block_gone=1`.\n- Fix idea: Treat a failed DELETE as an uncertain outcome. Re-fetch the rules and restore the block if it is absent, including when the response cannot be verified.\n\n### 2. Existing rule names are trusted without checking their contents or order\n- Severity: high\n- Confidence: high\n- Location: deploy/onecli-host-rules.sh:59\n- What happens: If an existing `pi-block-all` rule has been edited to allow traffic or target something other than `*`, the script accepts it as the catch-all block. Likewise, an existing allow rule with incorrect contents is skipped. When all expected names exist, the script also does not check whether the block comes after the allows, yet reports success.\n- Evidence: `has() { grep -qxF -- \"$1\" <<<\"$names\"; }` and `select(.name==$n) | .id`\n- Fix idea: Validate each matching rule’s action and target, and verify the effective ordering before reporting success. Repair mismatches or stop with an explicit error.\n\n### 3. Dry runs unnecessarily require the admin key\n- Severity: low\n- Confidence: high\n- Location: deploy/onecli-host-rules.sh:36\n- What happens: An operator without access to the Kubernetes secret or `ONECLI_API_KEY` cannot see the dry-run plan, even though dry run makes no OneCLI request and does not use the key.\n- Evidence: Key retrieval and the nonempty-key check happen before `if [[ $apply -eq 1 ]]`.\n- Fix idea: Retrieve and validate the admin key only in apply mode.\n\nEND-OF-REPORT"}]}
DONE
